---
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro'
import CompareTable from '../components/CompareTable.astro'
import { GITHUB_URL, SITE_URL } from '../consts.js'

const title = 'tldraw vs Excalidraw (vs Incantly Canvas): which whiteboard SDK in 2026?'
const description =
  'tldraw vs Excalidraw compared for embedding a whiteboard: licensing, React requirements, React Native support, sync, and pricing — plus where Incantly Canvas fits as the MIT third option.'

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'WebPage',
      name: title,
      url: `${SITE_URL}/tldraw-vs-excalidraw/`,
      description,
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is tldraw or Excalidraw better for embedding a whiteboard?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'tldraw offers deeper SDK polish under a custom license with a paid tier; Excalidraw is MIT and free but React-only with a smaller embedding API. Incantly Canvas is a third option: MIT, framework-free core, and React Native support.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is tldraw free for commercial use?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: "tldraw's SDK can be used commercially, but production use without attribution requires a paid business license. Check tldraw.dev for current terms.",
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is Excalidraw free for commercial use?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Yes, Excalidraw is MIT-licensed. Its embeddable component targets React apps.',
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
}
---
<Landing title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>tldraw vs Excalidraw — and the third option</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      You need a whiteboard in your product and the shortlist writes itself:
      tldraw or Excalidraw. They're both good. They also make different
      trade-offs — and depending on your stack, the right answer might be
      neither.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pick tldraw</strong> if you want the deepest SDK on the market, you're in React, and the custom license (with its paid business tier) works for your budget.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick Excalidraw</strong> if you want MIT-licensed software with a big community, you're in React, and its hand-drawn style plus element set covers your needs.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick Incantly Canvas</strong> if you want MIT licensing <em>and</em> need to run outside React — a plain web page, a custom framework, or React Native — or you want sync primitives without a dedicated server product.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Feature by feature</h2>
    <CompareTable />
    <p class="fine-print">
      Reflects public docs as of August 2026 — see
      <a href="https://tldraw.dev/community/license" rel="noopener nofollow">tldraw's license</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw" rel="noopener nofollow">Excalidraw's repo</a>.
      Spot an error? <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues`}>Tell us and we'll fix it.</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Licensing, in plain words</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>tldraw</strong> moved from MIT to a custom license in 2024. You
      can build and ship with it, but production use without its attribution
      requires a paid business license. That's a reasonable way to fund an
      excellent SDK — just budget for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Excalidraw</strong> is MIT. No fees at any scale. The
      constraint is technical rather than legal: the embeddable package is
      built for React, and collaboration runs through its room server.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Incantly Canvas</strong> is MIT too, packages for React, React
      Native, and the bare web, and keeps sync in the data model — every
      change is a JSON diff you can ship over any transport. It's the
      youngest of the three; what it has is complete, and it's
      <a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener">built in the open</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Questions that decide it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Are you in React?</strong> No → tldraw and Excalidraw are out; Incantly Canvas's core is framework-free.</li>
      <li><strong>Do you need mobile-native?</strong> Only Incantly Canvas ships a React Native package.</li>
      <li><strong>Is the budget zero?</strong> Excalidraw and Incantly Canvas are MIT; tldraw has a paid tier you may eventually need.</li>
      <li><strong>Do you need advanced canvas features today</strong> (frames, plugins, hosted sync)? tldraw is furthest ahead.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="more-links">
      More: <a href="/tldraw-alternative/">tldraw alternative</a> ·
      <a href="/excalidraw-alternative/">Excalidraw alternative</a> ·
      <a href="/blog/tldraw-vs-excalidraw-vs-incantly-canvas/">the long-form comparison</a> ·
      <a href="/docs/">Incantly Canvas docs</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Landing>
